{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.ExtensionClass","lean:module:QOU.GroundRingCore","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Span","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.groundRing_not_splits_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.groundRing_not_splits_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L214-L223","doc":"The normalisation-A non-split extension at `q = -1`, over the ground ring.\n\nNo hypothesis beyond `q = -1`: nontriviality of `R_q` is supplied by\ninstance search, so this is a statement about the actual ground ring rather\nthan about some ring that might be trivial. It is still a statement about\nnormalisation A at a root of unity, which `def:substrate-parameter` excludes\n— not about any `q` a QOU admits. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.hecke_not_splits_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.hecke_not_splits_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L195-L201","doc":"Under normalisation A the extension is non-split exactly at `q = -1` — the\ndouble-root point of `(T − q)(T + 1)`, hence the non-semisimple point.\n\nStated for `c = 1`, the generator of the classification. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.span_eq_q_add_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.span_eq_q_add_one","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L189-L193","doc":"Under normalisation A, `(T − q)(T + 1) = 0`, the classifying ideal is\n`(q + 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.not_splits_of_eq_of_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.not_splits_of_eq_of_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L169-L179","doc":"**The degenerate half.** Where the eigenvalues collide the ideal is zero,\nso `c` classifies on the nose and every nonzero `c` is a non-split\nextension.\n\n`α = β` is exactly where `(T − α)(T − β)` acquires a double root, i.e.\nwhere the algebra stops being semisimple. The extensions live there and\nnowhere else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.splits_of_isUnit_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.splits_of_isUnit_sub","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L157-L167","doc":"**The semisimple half.** If the two eigenvalues differ by a unit, every\nextension splits — the ideal is everything and the quotient is zero.\n\nThis is why `Ext¹ = 0` at the physical point: `q₀ ≈ 1.1099` makes\n`q₀ + 1 ≈ 2.11` invertible, and `skein-resolution-obstruction`'s\n`Rad = 0` is the same fact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.splits_iff_mem_span","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.splits_iff_mem_span","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L142-L155","doc":"**The classification.** `M_c` splits exactly when `c` lies in the ideal\ngenerated by `α − β`.\n\nThe section must be `y ↦ (λ y, y)` for some `λ`, and equivariance forces\n`α λ + c = λ β`. So the splittings are parametrised by solutions of\n`c = λ(β − α)`, and the extension classes by `R / (α − β)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.Splits","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.Splits","kind":"def","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L138-L140","doc":"The extension **splits** when the quotient generator lifts equivariantly:\na section `y ↦ (λ y, y)` commuting with `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L123-L136","doc":"**Every `c` gives a genuine extension.**\n\n`(T − α)(T − β) = 0` holds for all `c`, so the Hecke quadratic relation\nplaces no constraint on the extension parameter. This is what makes the\nclassification a full quotient of `R` rather than of a subset. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction_apply","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L120-L121","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HeckeExtOne.extAction","kind":"def","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HeckeExtOne.lean#L102-L118","doc":"**The rank-two extension** `M_c` of `V_β` by `V_α`.\n\nCarrier `R × R`, with `e₁ = (1,0)` spanning the sub-object `V_α` and\n`e₂ = (0,1)` lifting `V_β`. The generator acts by\n\n    T (x, y) = (α x + c y, β y).\n\nRead off the matrix: `T e₁ = α e₁` and `T e₂ = c e₁ + β e₂`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}